WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.405
An AI homelab produces and consumes many kinds of data: prompts, outputs, datasets, documents,

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:09.194
generated files, embeddings, model artifacts, logs, screenshots, notebooks, and configuration.

00:00:09.194 --> 00:00:14.120
Some of that data may be sensitive even if the lab is personal.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.910
Prompts can include names, internal notes, source code, personal records,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.047
or client information.

00:00:19.047 --> 00:00:21.699
Outputs can repeat or transform that information.

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:24.731
Logs can capture paths, tokens, errors, and identifiers.

00:00:24.731 --> 00:00:25.867
Organize data intentionally.

00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:30.793
Separate personal data, client data, experiments, public test data, and stable project assets.

00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:35.341
Use clear folders for models, datasets, outputs, temporary processing, and archived results.

00:00:35.341 --> 00:00:37.993
This makes backup and deletion decisions easier.

00:00:37.993 --> 00:00:43.298
It also reduces the chance that a public demo, shared screenshot, cloud sync folder,

00:00:43.298 --> 00:00:47.466
or repository commit accidentally includes information that should have stayed private.

00:00:47.466 --> 00:00:52.393
Backups should focus on the assets that are hard to recreate: configuration, notes,

00:00:52.393 --> 00:00:56.940
prompts you need to preserve, curated datasets, application code, and workflow definitions.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.971
Large model files can often be downloaded again,

00:00:59.971 --> 00:01:03.382
while carefully prepared datasets or notebooks may be unique.

00:01:03.382 --> 00:01:07.550
Encryption at rest may be appropriate when sensitive information is stored.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.444
Retention and deletion matter too.

00:01:09.444 --> 00:01:14.749
Keeping every experiment forever can turn a learning lab into a growing privacy liability.

00:01:14.749 --> 00:01:17.402
Treat the homelab as a data-handling environment.

00:01:17.402 --> 00:01:21.191
Decide what data is allowed, where it can be stored,

00:01:21.191 --> 00:01:24.981
whether cloud sync is acceptable, who can access shared folders,

00:01:24.981 --> 00:01:27.633
and how outputs are reviewed before sharing.

00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:31.044
Avoid putting secrets in prompts, logs, notebooks, or screenshots.

00:01:31.044 --> 00:01:35.970
Local AI can improve privacy compared with sending everything to a cloud service,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.380
but only if the local environment is managed carefully.
